# PromptForge Methodology Rule

This project uses PromptForge for requirements refinement before implementation.

## When to Refine

Before implementing any feature or fix, use the appropriate refinement command:

- `/refine-build` — General software development (default)
- `/refine-architect` — System design or structural changes
- `/refine-security` — Security-sensitive features
- `/refine-performance` — Performance optimization or critical paths
- `/refine-frontend` — User interface or client-side work
- `/refine-debug` — Bug investigation and diagnosis

## Refinement Rules

1. Always run refinement before implementation for non-trivial changes
2. The refined prompt must contain: objective, assumptions, scope, non-goals, acceptance criteria, testing requirements, implementation phases, and verification commands
3. All assumptions must be explicitly labeled
4. All open questions must be surfaced before implementation
5. Wait for `approve` before implementing
6. If the developer provides feedback instead of `approve`, revise the refinement

## Quality Standards

- Acceptance criteria must be specific and testable
- Scope and non-goals must be explicitly defined
- Repository context must be inspected for existing projects
- No invented architecture or unnecessary dependencies
